/P 1097 0 R
/E (We use our eyes to see the colour, shape and size of things. We use our hands to feel whether something is rough, smooth, hard or soft. We use our ears to hear sounds, and we use our nose to identify some smells.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1097 0 R
/E (For example, when we see a tree, we can observe that it has leaves, branches and a stem. We know that the tree is a living thing because it grows and needs water and air.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1097 0 R
/E (When we see a chair, we can observe that it has legs and a seat. We know that the chair is a non-living thing because it does not grow or need food and water.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1097 0 R
/E (Different things can have different features. Some things are big, while others are small. Some are heavy, while others are light. Some are smooth, while others are rough. By observing these features, we can identify and group things correctly.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1397 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1097 0 R
